.TH CHTTPSVR_RESP_SET_HEADER 3 "2026-09-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_resp_set_header \- set a response header, replacing every field of that name
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_resp_set_header(chttpsvr_resp *resp,
                                        const char *name,
                                        const char *value);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Sets a response header to one value.  If fields with that name
already exist, the first one takes the new value and keeps its
position and the others are removed, so the response ends up with
exactly one field of that name.  Names are compared without regard
to case, as RFC 7230 requires.  To send one more field of a name
instead, such as a second Set-Cookie, use chttpsvr_resp_add_header(3).
The fields go on the wire in the order of the calls that first set
each of them.

The server adds no Content-Type of its own.  A response carries one
only when the handler sets it, with this function or with
chttpsvr_resp_write_json(3).  The server doesn't guess one from the
body as Go's net/http server does, and it has no default type like
the default_type of nginx.

The stored header name keeps the letter case of the FIRST call, and
the letter case of every later call is ignored.  HTTP/1.1 header
names are case-insensitive on the wire, so this doesn't affect
correctness, but a caller that reads the stored header names back
directly has to allow for it.

None of the three arguments may be NULL.  A NULL in any of them gives
ccol_invalid_args and changes nothing in the response.  name must
also be a non-empty token of tchar bytes only, as RFC 7230 SS3.2.6
defines them, so every byte must be an ASCII letter, an ASCII digit
or one of "!#$%&'*+-.^_`|~".  A name of zero length has no valid form
on the wire.  A name with any other byte, for example a space or a
literal ':', can't inject a CRLF on its own, but it does make a
malformed wire line that a strict parser further down the path can
misread.

value must contain no control byte, which means no byte below 0x20
other than the horizontal tab and no 0x7F (DEL).  RFC 9110 section
5.5 allows only visible characters, the space, the tab and bytes from
0x80 to 0xFF in a field value, and a client that follows it,
chttpclient included, refuses a response with any other byte.  The
server writes name and value onto the wire exactly as they are, as
"name:value\er\en", with no further escaping.  A CR or LF inside
value would therefore let whoever controls the data that the caller
reflects (a query parameter, a path parameter or an echoed request
header put into a response header, for example) inject extra header
lines or split the response in two.  That attack is called HTTP
response splitting, or CRLF injection.  The function refuses such a
byte with ccol_invalid_args instead of quietly stripping it or
cutting the value short.

A "Connection" header is accepted and checked like any other name,
but the server never sends it.  The server always writes its own
Connection header, which shows whether it really keeps the
connection open afterwards.

A "Content-Length" value must be one or more ASCII digits whose
number fits in a signed 64-bit integer, and any other value gives
ccol_invalid_args.  Whether the server sends it depends on the
response.  A response that sends a body carries the length of the
body that chttpsvr_resp_write and its sibling functions really
wrote, never the value you set, because that length is the framing
on the wire.  A 304, and a response to HEAD where the handler wrote
no body, send no body whatever the field says.  They carry the
Content-Length you set, which is the length that a 200 to a GET would
carry (RFC 9110 section 8.6), or none if you set none.  A 205 always
carries "content-length:0", whatever you set or wrote, because it has
no content (RFC 9110 section 15.3.6), and a 204 never carries one.
Go's net/http server follows the same rule.

A "Date" header that you set is sent exactly as given and takes the
place of the Date that the server adds otherwise, so the response
carries exactly one.  If you set none, every final response carries
the current time in UTC in the IMF-fixdate form of RFC 9110 section
5.6.7, such as "Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT".  The interim
"100 Continue" carries none.  This is also the rule of Go's net/http
server.  A second call with the same name, in any letter case,
through this function or through chttpsvr_resp_add_header(3),
replaces the value, so no sequence of calls can produce two Date
lines.

A name of "Transfer-Encoding", in any letter case, is refused
outright instead of being accepted and filtered out later.  The
server never transfer-codes a response body, since it frames every
response with Content-Length, so it can't obey a Transfer-Encoding
header that a caller sets.  On the wire such a header would also sit
next to the Content-Length header that this function computes, over
a body that nothing transfer-coded.  That framing is ambiguous
(RFC 7230 SS3.3.3), and an intermediary that gives Transfer-Encoding
priority over Content-Length can misparse it, which is the same kind
of hazard that the handling of Connection and Content-Length above
prevents.  On the client side chttp_request_set_header refuses
Transfer-Encoding in the same way.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when resp, name or value is NULL; when name is
empty or holds a byte outside the RFC 7230 tchar set; when value
holds a byte below 0x20 other than the tab, or the byte 0x7F; when
name is "Transfer-Encoding" in any letter case; or when name is
"Content-Length" and value is not a number as described above.
ccol_not_enough_memory when the underlying allocation fails.
A failed call changes nothing in the response.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_resp_add_header (3),
.BR chttpsvr_resp_set_status (3),
.BR chttpsvr_resp_write (3)
